          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "The results I retrieved don't describe the main types or amounts of activity recommended for people with type 2 diabetes. They only include part of the \"Physical activity for type 2 diabetes\" summary, which covers precautions. It says:\n\n- **Eye disease (proliferative retinopathy):** Vigorous, high-impact, or straining activity may be restricted because of bleeding risk.\n- **Nerve damage in the feet (peripheral neuropathy):** Foot care, suitable footwear, and daily foot checks matter. Cycling or swimming may be preferred if there is a foot ulcer or reduced feeling in the feet.\n- **Nerve damage affecting heart rate (autonomic neuropathy):** Heart rate may not reliably show how hard you are working, so the summary suggests using how hard the activity feels instead.\n- **Very high blood sugar with ketones:** Activity should wait until this is corrected.\n\nA separate document, \"Physical activity for high blood pressure,\" gives general adult targets of 150 minutes a week of moderate activity (such as brisk walking or swimming) or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, plus muscle-strengthening on two or more days. That document is about blood pressure, not diabetes, so I can't confirm those targets apply to diabetes here.\n\nBefore starting or changing an activity plan, it's worth asking a clinician which activities suit you, especially if any of the conditions above apply to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
